with q as (
  select $l21_quiz$[{"id": "q1", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The chapter calls radiation 'the wall' \u2014 different in kind from vacuum, cold, and missing air. What is the distinction it draws?", "options": ["Radiation is simply more lethal than the other threats", "Vacuum, cold, and missing air are solved engineering problems (a sealed, heated, oxygenated hull ends them); radiation has no clean technical solution, so it converts the mission from 'can we build it?' into 'how much irreversible harm will we accept?'", "Radiation only matters near Mars, while the other threats matter everywhere", "The other threats can be felt and radiation cannot, but otherwise they are the same kind of problem"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Vacuum, cold, and missing air are eliminated by building a pressurised, warmed, oxygenated vessel \u2014 solved problems. Radiation is not: the galactic dose cannot be fully shielded (the spallation paradox plus the rocket-equation mass penalty) and cannot be adapted to, so no design makes it go away. What is left is not a solution but a decision \u2014 accept a measured cancer-risk increase and unknown cognitive risk \u2014 which turns an engineering question into a moral one. That shift, from a problem you solve to a harm you accept, is why it reads as a wall."}, {"id": "q2", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Solar particle events (SPEs) and galactic cosmic rays (GCRs) demand opposite strategies. Which pairing is correct?", "options": ["SPEs are chronic and unshieldable; GCRs are acute and shieldable", "Both are shieldable behind a few centimetres of polyethylene, so a single shelter defends against both", "SPEs are acute, sporadic MeV-proton bursts that a shelter CAN stop; GCRs are constant GeV heavy nuclei from every direction that are near-unshieldable, so you reduce and accept them", "SPEs come from the galaxy and GCRs come from the Sun"], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "The solar storm is an acute burst of moderate-energy protons \u2014 it can kill quickly, but moderate protons are stopped by a modest shelter, so the crew retreats and waits it out. Galactic cosmic rays are a constant rain of extreme-energy heavy nuclei from all directions; there is no storm to wait out and no practical shield, so the dose simply accumulates and is largely accepted. Confusing them leads to the wrong defence: a shelter solves only the solar half, and nothing cheaply solves the galactic half."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why can adding a MODERATE amount of shielding make the galactic-cosmic-ray dose WORSE than no shielding at all?", "options": ["The shield traps heat, which amplifies radiation damage", "A high-energy nucleus striking the shield undergoes spallation \u2014 it shatters into a shower of secondary particles (neutrons, protons, fragments); a moderate shield makes the shrapnel without being thick enough to absorb it, so the internal dose rises before it falls", "Shielding attracts more cosmic rays by its mass", "The shield becomes radioactive and emits its own dose forever"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "A GeV nucleus does not gently stop; it collides violently and shatters both itself and a nucleus in the shield (spallation), spraying secondary neutrons, protons, and fragments onward into the crew. Those many secondaries \u2014 numerous, well-matched to depositing damage in tissue, and rich in biologically nasty neutrons \u2014 can do more total harm than the single original particle. A moderate shield produces the shower without being thick enough to absorb it, so the dose climbs above the no-shield line, peaks, and only falls once the shield grows enormous. A little is worse than none; the moderate middle is the worst place to be."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "For galactic cosmic rays, hydrogen is the best shielding material \u2014 the reverse of the lead-for-X-rays rule. Why?", "options": ["Hydrogen is transparent to all radiation, so it lets the particles pass harmlessly", "Hydrogen is cheap and abundant, so more of it can be flown", "Hydrogen packs the most electrons per unit mass to bleed off energy, and \u2014 because equal-mass collisions transfer energy most cleanly \u2014 a proton stops another proton efficiently while producing almost no nuclear shrapnel, whereas heavy lead nuclei shatter into copious secondaries", "Hydrogen's magnetic properties deflect the nuclei like a miniature magnetosphere"], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "Two effects favour the lightest element. First, per unit mass hydrogen carries the most electrons, and it is electrons that drain a charged particle's energy. Second, a head-on collision between equal masses transfers energy most completely, so a proton stops an incoming proton far better than a massive lead nucleus does \u2014 and, having only a single proton for a nucleus, hydrogen produces almost no spallation shrapnel. Heavy nuclei do the opposite, shattering into secondary spray. So water and polyethylene beat aluminium, and aluminium beats lead \u2014 exactly reversing the rule for X-rays, which are stopped by dense electron clouds."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The RAD instrument on Curiosity measured ~1.8 mSv/day in deep space. Against a ~600 mSv career limit, why is 'go faster' \u2014 not 'add shielding' \u2014 the countermeasure the chapter emphasises, and how does it tie to a 1973 cancellation?", "options": ["Faster travel lets the crew outrun the cosmic rays, which move slower than the ship", "The galactic dose is proportional to time exposed, so halving the transit roughly halves the transit dose; since no shield scales against GCRs, shortening the trip is the strongest lever \u2014 which makes it a propulsion problem, and the NERVA nuclear engine, ground-tested and cancelled in 1973, was in effect a medical device", "Faster travel raises the ship's own magnetic field, deflecting the particles", "Going faster reduces the number of solar storms the Sun produces"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Because the galactic dose is a rate, its total is set by how long you are out there: a round trip banks ~1 Sv, some 67% over a whole career limit, and no affordable shield stops the bombardment. The one lever that scales is time \u2014 halve the transit and you roughly halve the transit dose. Halving the transit is a propulsion problem, so radiation is partly an engine problem. The United States built and ground-tested a nuclear thermal engine (NERVA) in the 1960s that could have shortened the trip, then cancelled it in 1973 for want of a mission \u2014 an engine that is, in effect, a piece of medical equipment."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "Explain why leaving Earth removes 'the only radiation protection our species has ever had,' naming the two shields and the mechanism of each, and why the same numbers make the ISS a poor test of deep-space radiation. Use the dose-rate hierarchy (surface, ISS, deep space).", "rubric": "THE TWO SHIELDS AND THEIR MECHANISMS: (1) the MAGNETOSPHERE \u2014 Earth's magnetic field, generated by its molten-iron core and reaching tens of thousands of km into space, protects by DEFLECTING charged particles: most dangerous space radiation is charged (protons, nuclei), and a charge crossing a magnetic field feels a sideways force that curves its path, steering the particles around the planet (and funnelling some to the poles, lighting the auroras). (2) the ATMOSPHERE \u2014 a thick ABSORBER, ~1,000 g/cm\u00b2 of material (\u224810 m of water equivalent), which soaks up the particles and photons that get past the field, dissipating their energy in cascades high overhead. WHY BOTH ARE LEFT BEHIND: both are properties of the PLANET \u2014 a spacecraft can carry neither a planetary magnetic field nor a ten-metre water column \u2014 so a deep-space crew has only the ship's thin walls. THE DOSE HIERARCHY: Earth's surface ~3 mSv/year (the background all life evolved in); the ISS ~50\u00d7 the surface rate; deep space ~1.8 mSv/day, ~200\u00d7 the surface rate, continuously. WHY THE ISS IS A POOR TEST: the ISS is in low Earth orbit, STILL INSIDE the magnetosphere, so it is substantially protected \u2014 its dose rate is far below deep space's despite already being ~50\u00d7 the surface. Thus ISS experience understates the deep-space environment, which is why a Mars-transit crew, outside the field entirely, faces a categorically worse exposure than any ISS increment. A strong answer stresses that the danger of space IS the absence of these two invisible, immensely effective shields."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "A Mars round trip was measured near 1 Sv against a ~600 mSv career limit. Trace how a measured dose becomes a decision, covering: the RAD numbers and where they came from; why the sievert (not the gray) is the honest unit for heavy nuclei; the realistic strategies and what each can and cannot do; and why the chapter says radiation converts an engineering problem into a moral one.", "rubric": "THE MEASURED NUMBERS AND SOURCE: these are not estimates \u2014 the RADIATION ASSESSMENT DETECTOR (RAD) aboard the Curiosity rover measured the real dose on the real 2011\u201312 cruise to Mars and on the surface: ~1.8 mSv/day in transit, ~648 mSv over a ~240-day one-way cruise, ~0.35 Sv on the surface, ~1 Sv for a round trip \u2014 against a ~600 mSv career limit set to hold excess fatal-cancer risk near a few percent, so one trip runs ~67% OVER a whole career's allowance. GRAY VS SIEVERT: the gray is physical (joules per kg); the sievert multiplies it by a weighting factor for biological harm (~1 for X-rays/electrons, ~2 protons, ~5\u201320 neutrons, up to ~20 for the heavy nuclei of GCRs) \u2014 because a heavy nucleus deposits energy in a DENSE track (clustered DNA damage the cell cannot repair) while an X-ray deposits it thinly. So the sievert is honest for GCRs precisely where the gray misleads, and every dose figure quoted is already biologically weighted \u2014 which is why a modest energy deposition becomes a career's worth of risk. STRATEGIES AND THEIR LIMITS: (1) STORM SHELTER \u2014 stops the acute SPE (moderate protons are shieldable), useless against GCRs; (2) CONSUMABLES AS SHIELDING \u2014 water/food/waste (hydrogen-rich) arranged around the bunks shave the GCR dose 'for free' (mass already on the manifest) but only partially, and full-habitat shielding is too heavy; (3) GO FASTER \u2014 since GCR dose \u221d time, halving the transit ~halves the transit dose; the strongest lever, but it requires advanced (nuclear) propulsion and does nothing for the acute SPE; (4) timing/mission design \u2014 fly at solar max (more solar wind lowers GCR flux, but raises SPE risk), regolith shelters on the surface. ALL only REDUCE, never eliminate. THE MORAL SHIFT: because radiation cannot be escaped, fully shielded, adapted to, or felt, no design makes it go away \u2014 you reduce it to some level and then someone must DECIDE that level is acceptable and ask crews to bear the residual (a few-percent excess cancer risk plus unknown cognitive/cardiac/ocular risk). That turns 'can we build it?' (an engineering yes/no) into 'how much irreversible harm will we impose on the crews we send?' (a moral question). A robot arrives unchanged; a human arrives with damage no shield fully prevented and no exercise can undo. That shift \u2014 from a problem you solve to a harm you accept \u2014 is what makes radiation a wall, and a strong answer ties it to the through-line: never send a human where a machine would do unless what the human brings is worth THAT price."}]$l21_quiz$::jsonb as questions
